## an3.63
an3.63:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was wandering in the land of the Kosalans together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants when he arrived at a village of the Kosalan brahmins named Venāgapura. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was wandering on tour among the Kosalans together with a large Saṅgha of bhikkhus when he reached the Kosalan brahmin village named Venāgapura.
an3.63:1.2 | S: The brahmins and householders of Venāgapura heard: | T: The brahmin householders of Venāgapura heard:
an3.63:1.3 | S: “It seems the ascetic Gotama—a Sakyan, gone forth from a Sakyan family—has arrived at Venāgapura. | T: “It is said that the ascetic Gotama, the son of the Sakyans who went forth from a Sakyan family, has arrived at Venāgapura.
an3.63:1.4 | S: He has this good reputation: | T: Now a good report about that Master Gotama has circulated thus:
an3.63:1.5 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘That Blessed One is an arahant, perfectly enlightened, accomplished in true knowledge and conduct, fortunate, knower of the world, unsurpassed leader of persons to be tamed, teacher of devas and humans, the Enlightened One, the Blessed One.
an3.63:1.6 | S: He has realized with his own insight this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, gods and humans—and he makes it known to others. | T: Having realized by his own direct knowledge this world with its devas, Māra, and Brahmā, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its devas and humans, he makes it known to others.
an3.63:1.7 | S: He teaches Dhamma that’s good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased. And he reveals a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. | T: He teaches a Dhamma that is good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, with the right meaning and phrasing; he reveals a spiritual life that is perfectly complete and pure.’
an3.63:1.8 | S: It’s good to see such perfected ones.” | T: Now it is good to see such arahants.”
an3.63:2.1 | S: Then the brahmins and householders of Venāgapura went up to the Buddha. Before sitting down to one side, some bowed, some exchanged greetings and polite conversation, some held up their cupped palms toward the Buddha, some announced their name and clan, while some kept silent. Then the brahmin Vacchagotta of Venāgapura said to the Buddha: | T: Then the brahmin householders of Venāgapura approached the Blessed One. Some paid homage to the Blessed One and sat down to one side; some exchanged greetings with him and, when they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, sat down to one side; some reverentially saluted him and sat down to one side; some pronounced their name and clan and sat down to one side; some kept silent and sat down to one side. The brahmin Vacchagotta of Venāgapura then said to the Blessed One:
an3.63:3.1 | S: “It’s incredible, worthy Gotama, it’s amazing, | T: “It is astounding and amazing, Master Gotama,
an3.63:3.2 | S: how your faculties are so very clear, and the complexion of your skin is pure and bright. | T: how Master Gotama’s faculties are tranquil and the color of his skin is pure and bright.
an3.63:3.3 | S: It’s like a golden brown jujube in the autumn, | T: Just as a yellow jujube fruit in the autumn is pure and bright,
an3.63:3.4 | P: evamevaṁ bhoto gotamassa vippasannāni indriyāni parisuddho chavivaṇṇo pariyodāto. | T: so Master Gotama’s faculties are tranquil and the color of his skin is pure and bright.
an3.63:3.5 | S: or a ripe palmyra fruit freshly plucked from the stalk, | T: Just as a palm fruit that has just been removed from its stalk is pure and bright,
an3.63:3.6 | P: evamevaṁ bhoto gotamassa vippasannāni indriyāni parisuddho chavivaṇṇo pariyodāto. | T: so Master Gotama’s faculties are tranquil and the color of his skin is pure and bright.
an3.63:3.7 | S: or a pendant of Black Plum River gold, fashioned by a deft smith, well-wrought in the forge, and placed on a cream rug where it shines and glows and dazzles. | T: Just as an ornament of finest gold, well prepared by a skilled goldsmith and very skillfully wrought in the furnace, placed on red brocade, shines and beams and radiates,
an3.63:3.8 | S: In the same way, your faculties are so very clear, and the complexion of your skin is pure and bright. | T: so Master Gotama’s faculties are tranquil and the color of his skin is pure and bright.
an3.63:3.9 | S: Surely the worthy Gotama gets when he wants, without trouble or difficulty, various kinds of high and luxurious bedding, such as: | T: “Whatever high and luxurious kinds of bedding there are—that is,
an3.63:3.10 | S: sofas, couches, woolen covers—shag-piled, colorful, white, embroidered with flowers, quilted, embroidered with animals, double or single fringed—and silk covers studded with gems, as well as silken sheets, wollen carpets, rugs for elephants, horses, or chariots, black antelope hides and woven rugs, and spreads of fine deer hide, with a canopy above and red cushions at both ends.” | T: a sofa, a divan, a long-haired coverlet, a coverlet of diverse colors, a white coverlet, a woolen coverlet with floral designs, a quilt of cotton wool, a woolen coverlet ornamented with animal figures, a woolen coverlet with double borders, a woolen coverlet with a single border, a silken sheet studded with gems, a sheet made with silk threads and studded with gems, a dancer’s rug, an elephant rug, a horse rug, a chariot rug, a rug of antelope hide, a spread made of the hide of the kadali-deer, a bed with a canopy above and red bolsters at both ends—Master Gotama surely gains them at will, without trouble or difficulty.”
an3.63:4.1 | S: “Brahmin, these various kinds of high and luxurious bedding | T: “Brahmin, those high and luxurious kinds of bedding
an3.63:4.2 | P: āsandi pallaṅko gonako cittako paṭikā paṭalikā tūlikā vikatikā uddalomī ekantalomī kaṭṭissaṁ koseyyaṁ kuttakaṁ hatthattharaṁ assattharaṁ rathattharaṁ ajinappaveṇī kadalimigapavarapaccattharaṇaṁ sauttaracchadaṁ ubhatolohitakūpadhānaṁ. | T: (none)
an3.63:4.3 | S: are hard for renunciates to find. And even if they do get them, they’re not allowed. | T: are rarely obtained by those who have gone forth, and if they are obtained, they are not allowed.
an3.63:5.1 | S: There are, brahmin, these three high and luxurious beds that I get these days when I want, without trouble or difficulty. | T: “But, brahmin, there are three kinds of high and luxurious beds that at present I gain at will, without trouble or difficulty.
an3.63:5.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.63:5.3 | S: The heavenly high and luxurious bed, the divine high and luxurious bed, and the noble high and luxurious bed. | T: The celestial high and luxurious bed, the divine high and luxurious bed, and the noble high and luxurious bed.
an3.63:5.4 | S: These are the three high and luxurious beds that I get these days when I want, without trouble or difficulty.” | T: These are the three kinds of high and luxurious beds that at present I gain at will, without trouble or difficulty.”
an3.63:6.1 | S: “But what, worthy Gotama, is the heavenly high and luxurious bed?” | T: (1) “But, Master Gotama, what is the celestial high and luxurious bed that at present you gain at will, without trouble or difficulty?”
an3.63:6.2 | S: “Brahmin, when I am living supported by a village or town, I robe up in the morning and, taking my bowl and robe, enter the town or village for alms. | T: “Here, brahmin, when I am dwelling in dependence on a village or town, in the morning I dress, take my bowl and robe, and enter that village or town for alms.
an3.63:6.3 | S: After the meal, on my return from almsround, I enter within a forest. | T: After the meal, when I have returned from the alms round, I enter a grove.
an3.63:6.4 | S: I gather up some grass or leaves into a pile and sit down cross-legged, setting my body straight, and bringing mindfulness to the present. | T: I collect some grass or leaves that I find there into a pile and then sit down. Having folded my legs crosswise and straightened my body, I establish mindfulness in front of me.
an3.63:6.5 | S: Quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, I enter and remain in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: Then, secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unwholesome states, I enter and dwell in the first jhāna, which consists of rapture and pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by thought and examination.
an3.63:6.6 | S: As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, I enter and remain in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: With the subsiding of thought and examination, I enter and dwell in the second jhāna, which has internal placidity and unification of mind and consists of rapture and pleasure born of concentration, without thought and examination.
an3.63:6.7 | S: And with the fading away of rapture, I enter and remain in the third absorption, where I meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ | T: With the fading away as well of rapture, I dwell equanimous and, mindful and clearly comprehending, I experience pleasure with the body; I enter and dwell in the third jhāna of which the noble ones declare: ‘He is equanimous, mindful, one who dwells happily.’
an3.63:6.8 | S: With the giving up of pleasure and pain, and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, I enter and remain in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. | T: With the abandoning of pleasure and pain, and with the previous passing away of joy and dejection, I enter and dwell in the fourth jhāna, neither painful nor pleasant, which has purification of mindfulness by equanimity.
an3.63:6.9 | S: When I’m practicing like this, if I walk, at that time my walking is heavenly. | T: “Then, brahmin, when I am in such a state, if I walk back and forth, on that occasion my walking back and forth is celestial.
an3.63:6.10 | S: When I’m practicing like this, if I stand, at that time my standing is heavenly. | T: If I am standing, on that occasion my standing is celestial.
an3.63:6.11 | S: When I’m practicing like this, if I sit, at that time my sitting is heavenly. | T: If I am sitting, on that occasion my sitting is celestial.
an3.63:6.12 | S: When I’m practicing like this, if I lie down, at that time my lying is heavenly. | T: If I lie down, on that occasion this is my celestial high and luxurious bed.
an3.63:6.13 | S: This is the heavenly high and luxurious bed that I get these days when I want, without trouble or difficulty.” | T: This is that celestial high and luxurious bed that at present I can gain at will, without trouble or difficulty.”
an3.63:7.1 | S: “It’s incredible, worthy Gotama, it’s amazing! | T: “It is astounding and amazing, Master Gotama!
an3.63:7.2 | S: Who but the worthy Gotama could get such a heavenly high and luxurious bed when he wants, without trouble or difficulty? | T: Who else, apart from Master Gotama, can gain at will, without trouble or difficulty, such a celestial high and luxurious bed?
an3.63:8.1 | S: But what, worthy Gotama, is the divine high and luxurious bed?” | T: (2) “But, Master Gotama, what is the divine high and luxurious bed that at present you gain at will, without trouble or difficulty?”
an3.63:8.2 | S: “Brahmin, when I am living supported by a village or town, I robe up in the morning and, taking my bowl and robe, enter the town or village for alms. | T: “Here, brahmin, when I am dwelling in dependence on a village or town, in the morning I dress, take my bowl and robe, and enter that village or town for alms.
an3.63:8.3 | S: After the meal, on my return from almsround, I enter within a forest. | T: After the meal, when I have returned from the alms round, I enter a grove.
an3.63:8.4 | S: I gather up some grass or leaves into a pile and sit down cross-legged, setting my body straight, and bringing mindfulness to the present. | T: I collect some grass or leaves that I find there into a pile and then sit down. Having folded my legs crosswise and straightened my body, I establish mindfulness in front of me.
an3.63:8.5 | S: I meditate spreading a heart full of love to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, I spread a heart full of love to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: Then I dwell pervading one quarter with a mind imbued with loving-kindness, likewise the second quarter, the third quarter, and the fourth quarter. Thus above, below, across, and everywhere, and to all as to myself, I dwell pervading the entire world with a mind imbued with loving-kindness, vast, exalted, measureless, without enmity, without ill will.
an3.63:8.6 | S: I meditate spreading a heart full of compassion to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, I spread a heart full of compassion to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: I dwell pervading one quarter with a mind imbued with compassion …
an3.63:8.7 | S: I meditate spreading a heart full of rejoicing to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, I spread a heart full of rejoicing to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: with a mind imbued with altruistic joy …
an3.63:8.8 | S: I meditate spreading a heart full of equanimity to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, I spread a heart full of equanimity to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: with a mind imbued with equanimity, likewise the second quarter, the third quarter, and the fourth quarter. Thus above, below, across, and everywhere, and to all as to myself, I dwell pervading the entire world with a mind imbued with equanimity, vast, exalted, measureless, without enmity, without ill will.
an3.63:8.9 | S: When I’m practicing like this, if I walk, at that time my walking is divine. | T: “Then, brahmin, when I am in such a state, if I walk back and forth, on that occasion my walking back and forth is divine.
an3.63:8.10 | S: … my standing is divine. | T: If I am standing, on that occasion my standing is divine.
an3.63:8.11 | S: … my sitting is divine. | T: If I am sitting, on that occasion my sitting is divine.
an3.63:8.12 | S: When I’m practicing like this, if I lie down, at that time my lying is divine. | T: If I lie down, on that occasion this is my divine high and luxurious bed.
an3.63:8.13 | S: This is the divine high and luxurious bed that I get these days when I want, without trouble or difficulty.” | T: This is that divine high and luxurious bed that at present I can gain at will, without trouble or difficulty.”
