## an3.6
an3.6:1.1 | S: “A fool is known by three things. | T: “Bhikkhus, one who possesses three qualities should be known as a fool.
an3.6:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.6:1.3 | S: Unskillful deeds by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: Unwholesome bodily action, unwholesome verbal action, and unwholesome mental action.
an3.6:1.4 | S: These are the three things by which a fool is known. | T: One who possesses these three qualities should be known as a fool.
an3.6:2.1 | S: An astute person is known by three things. | T: “One who possesses three qualities should be known as a wise person.
an3.6:2.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.6:2.3 | S: Skillful deeds by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: Wholesome bodily action, wholesome verbal action, and wholesome mental action.
an3.6:2.4 | S: These are the three things by which an astute person is known. | T: One who possesses these three qualities should be known as a wise person.
an3.6:2.5 | S: So you should train …” | T: “Therefore … It is in this way that you should train yourselves.”
an3.6:2.6 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.7
an3.7:1.1 | S: “A fool is known by three things. | T: “Bhikkhus, one who possesses three qualities should be known as a fool.
an3.7:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.7:1.3 | S: Blameworthy deeds by way of body, speech, and mind. … An astute person is known by blameless deeds by way of body, speech, and mind. …” | T: Blameworthy bodily action, blameworthy verbal action, and blameworthy mental action. One who possesses these three qualities should be known as a fool. “One who possesses three qualities should be known as a wise person. What three? Blameless bodily action, blameless verbal action, and blameless mental action. One who possesses these three qualities should be known as a wise person. “Therefore … It is in this way that you should train yourselves.”
an3.7:1.4 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.8
an3.8:1.1 | S: “A fool is known by three things. | T: “Bhikkhus, one who possesses three qualities should be known as a fool.
an3.8:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.8:1.3 | S: Hurtful deeds by way of body, speech, and mind. … An astute person is known by kind deeds by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: Afflictive bodily action, afflictive verbal action, and afflictive mental action. One who possesses these three qualities should be known as a fool. “One who possesses three qualities should be known as a wise person. What three? Unafflictive bodily action, unafflictive verbal action, and unafflictive mental action.
an3.8:1.4 | S: These are the three things by which an astute person is known. | T: One who possesses these three qualities should be known as a wise person.
an3.8:2.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Therefore, bhikkhus, you should train yourselves thus:
an3.8:2.2 | S: ‘We will shun the three qualities by which a fool is known, and we will proceed having undertaken the three qualities by which an astute person is known.’ | T: ‘We will avoid the three qualities possessing which one is known as a fool, and we will undertake and observe the three qualities possessing which one is known as a wise person.’
an3.8:2.3 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: It is in this way that you should train yourselves.”
an3.8:2.4 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.9
an3.9:1.1 | S: “When a foolish, incompetent, untrue person has three qualities they keep themselves broken and damaged. They deserve to be blamed and criticized by sensible people, and they brim with much wickedness. | T: “Bhikkhus, possessing three qualities, the foolish, incompetent, bad person maintains himself in a maimed and injured condition; he is blameworthy and subject to reproach by the wise; and he generates much demerit.
an3.9:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.9:1.3 | S: Bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: Bodily misconduct, verbal misconduct, and mental misconduct.
an3.9:1.4 | S: When a foolish, incompetent untrue person possesses these three qualities they keep themselves broken and damaged. They deserve to be blamed and criticized by sensible people, and they brim with much wickedness. | T: Possessing these three qualities, the foolish, incompetent, bad person maintains himself in a maimed and injured condition; he is blameworthy and subject to reproach by the wise; and he generates much demerit.
an3.9:2.1 | S: When an astute, competent true person has three qualities they keep themselves healthy and whole. They don’t deserve to be blamed and criticized by sensible people, and they brim with much merit. | T: “Bhikkhus, possessing three qualities, the wise, competent, good person preserves himself unmaimed and uninjured; he is blameless and beyond reproach by the wise; and he generates much merit.
an3.9:2.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.9:2.3 | S: Good conduct by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: Bodily good conduct, verbal good conduct, and mental good conduct.
an3.9:2.4 | S: When an astute, competent true person possesses these three qualities they keep themselves healthy and whole. They don’t deserve to be blamed and criticized by sensible people, and they brim with much merit.” | T: Possessing these three qualities, the wise, competent, good person preserves himself unmaimed and uninjured; he is blameless and beyond reproach by the wise; and he generates much merit.”
an3.9:2.5 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.10
an4.10:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these four yokes. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these four bonds.
an4.10:1.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an4.10:1.3 | S: The yokes of sensual pleasures, future lives, views, and ignorance. | T: The bond of sensuality, the bond of existence, the bond of views, and the bond of ignorance.
an4.10:1.4 | S: And what is the yoke of sensual pleasures? | T: (1) “And what, bhikkhus, is the bond of sensuality?
an4.10:1.5 | S: It’s when you don’t truly understand sensual pleasures’ origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape. | T: Here, someone does not understand as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in regard to sensual pleasures.
an4.10:1.6 | S: So greed, relishing, affection, infatuation, thirst, passion, attachment, and craving for sensual pleasures linger on inside. | T: When one does not understand these things as they really are, then sensual lust, sensual delight, sensual affection, sensual infatuation, sensual thirst, sensual passion, sensual attachment, and sensual craving lie deep within one in regard to sensual pleasures.
an4.10:1.7 | S: This is called the yoke of sensual pleasures. | T: This is called the bond of sensuality.
an4.10:1.8 | S: Such is the yoke of sensual pleasures. | T: (2) “Such is the bond of sensuality.
an4.10:2.1 | S: And what is the yoke of future lives? | T: And how is there the bond of existence?
an4.10:2.2 | S: It’s when you don’t truly understand future lives’ origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape. | T: Here, someone does not understand as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in regard to states of existence.
an4.10:2.3 | S: So greed, relishing, affection, infatuation, thirst, passion, attachment, and craving for continued existence linger on inside. | T: When one does not understand these things as they really are, then lust for existence, delight in existence, affection for existence, infatuation with existence, thirst for existence, passion for existence, attachment to existence, and craving for existence lie deep within one in regard to states of existence.
an4.10:2.4 | S: This is called the yoke of future lives. | T: This is called the bond of existence.
an4.10:2.5 | S: Such are the yokes of sensual pleasures and future lives. | T: (3)“Such are the bond of sensuality and the bond of existence.
an4.10:3.1 | S: And what is the yoke of views? | T: And how is there the bond of views?
an4.10:3.2 | S: It’s when you don’t truly understand views’ origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape. | T: Here, someone does not understand as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in regard to views.
an4.10:3.3 | S: So greed, relishing, affection, infatuation, thirst, passion, attachment, and craving for views linger on inside. | T: When one does not understand these things as they really are, then lust for views, delight in views, affection for views, infatuation with views, thirst for views, passion for views, attachment to views, and craving for views lie deep within one in regard to views.
an4.10:3.4 | S: This is called the yoke of views. | T: This is called the bond of views.
an4.10:3.5 | S: Such are the yokes of sensual pleasures, future lives, and views. | T: (4) “Such are the bond of sensuality, the bond of existence, and the bond of views.
an4.10:4.1 | S: And what is the yoke of ignorance? | T: And how is there the bond of ignorance?
an4.10:4.2 | S: It’s when you don’t truly understand the six fields of contact’s origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape, | T: Here, someone does not understand as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in regard to the six bases for contact.
an4.10:4.3 | S: so ignorance and unknowing of the six fields of contact linger on inside. | T: When one does not understand these things as they really are, then, ignorance and unknowing lie deep within one in regard to the six bases for contact.
an4.10:4.4 | S: This is called the yoke of ignorance. | T: This is called the bond of ignorance.
an4.10:4.5 | S: Such are the yokes of sensual pleasures, future lives, views, and ignorance. | T: Such are the bond of sensuality, the bond of existence, the bond of views, and the bond of ignorance.
an4.10:4.6 | S: These are bad, unskillful qualities that are corrupting, leading to future lives, hurtful, resulting in suffering and future rebirth, old age, and death. That’s why someone yoked to them is called: ‘one who has not found sanctuary from the yoke’. | T: “One is fettered by bad unwholesome states that are defiling, conducive to renewed existence, troublesome, ripening in suffering, leading to future birth, old age, and death; therefore one is said to be ‘not secure from bondage.’
an4.10:4.7 | S: These are the four yokes. | T: These are the four bonds.
an4.10:5.1 | S: There are these four kinds of unyoking. | T: “There are, bhikkhus, these four severances of bonds.
an4.10:5.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an4.10:5.3 | S: Unyoking from sensual pleasures, future lives, views, and ignorance. | T: The severance of the bond of sensuality, the severance of the bond of existence, the severance of the bond of views, and the severance of the bond of ignorance.
an4.10:5.4 | S: And what is unyoking from sensual pleasures? | T: (1) “And what, bhikkhus, is the severance of the bond of sensuality?
an4.10:5.5 | S: It’s when you truly understand sensual pleasures’ origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape. | T: Here, someone understands as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in regard to sensual pleasures.
an4.10:5.6 | S: So greed, relishing, affection, infatuation, thirst, passion, attachment, and craving for sensual pleasures don’t linger on inside. | T: When one understands these things as they really are, then sensual lust, sensual delight, sensual affection, sensual infatuation, sensual thirst, sensual passion, sensual attachment, and sensual craving do not lie within one in regard to sensual pleasures.
an4.10:5.7 | S: This is called unyoking from sensual pleasures. | T: This is called the severance of the bond of sensuality.
an4.10:5.8 | S: Such is unyoking from sensual pleasures. | T: (2) “Such is the severance of the bond of sensuality.
an4.10:6.1 | S: And what is unyoking from future lives? | T: And how is there the severance of the bond of existence?
an4.10:6.2 | S: It’s when you truly understand future lives’ origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape. | T: Here, someone understands as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in regard to states of existence.
an4.10:6.3 | S: So greed, relishing, affection, infatuation, thirst, passion, attachment, and craving for continued existence don’t linger on inside. | T: When one understands these things as they really are, then lust for existence, delight in existence, affection for existence, infatuation with existence, thirst for existence, passion for existence, attachment to existence, and craving for existence do not lie within one in regard to states of existence.
an4.10:6.4 | S: This is called unyoking from future lives. | T: This is called the severance of the bond of existence.
an4.10:6.5 | S: Such is unyoking from sensual pleasures and future lives. | T: (3) “Such are the severance of the bond of sensuality and the severance of the bond of existence.
an4.10:7.1 | S: And what is unyoking from views? | T: And how is there the severance of the bond of views?
an4.10:7.2 | S: It’s when you truly understand views’ origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape. | T: Here, someone understands as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in regard to views.
an4.10:7.3 | S: So greed, relishing, affection, infatuation, thirst, passion, attachment, and craving for views don’t linger on inside. | T: When one understands these things as they really are, then lust for views, delight in views, affection for views, infatuation with views, thirst for views, passion for views, attachment to views, and craving for views do not lie within one in regard to views.
an4.10:7.4 | S: This is called unyoking from views. | T: This is called the severance of the bond of views.
an4.10:7.5 | S: Such is unyoking from sensual pleasures, future lives, and views. | T: (4) “Such are the severance of the bond of sensuality, the severance of the bond of existence, and the severance of the bond of views.
an4.10:8.1 | S: And what is unyoking from ignorance? | T: And how is there the severance of the bond of ignorance?
an4.10:8.2 | S: It’s when you truly understand the six fields of contact’s origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape, | T: Here, someone understands as they really are the origin and the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in regard to the six bases for contact.
an4.10:8.3 | S: so ignorance and unknowing of the six fields of contact don’t linger on inside. | T: When one understands these things as they really are, then ignorance and unknowing do not lie within one in regard to the six bases for contact.
an4.10:8.4 | S: This is called unyoking from ignorance. | T: This is called the severance of the bond of ignorance.
an4.10:8.5 | S: Such is unyoking from sensual pleasures, future lives, views, and ignorance. | T: Such are the severance of the bond of sensuality, the severance of the bond of existence, the severance of the bond of views, and the severance of the bond of ignorance.
an4.10:8.6 | S: These are bad, unskillful qualities that are corrupting, leading to future lives, hurtful, resulting in suffering and future rebirth, old age, and death. That’s why someone unyoked from them is called: ‘one who has found sanctuary from the yoke’. | T: “One is detached from bad unwholesome states that are defiling, conducive to renewed existence, troublesome, ripening in suffering, leading to future birth, old age, and death; therefore one is said to be ‘secure from bondage.’
an4.10:8.7 | S: These are the four kinds of unyoking. | T: These are the four severances of bonds.”
an4.10:9.1 | S: Yoked to both sensual pleasures | T: Fettered by the bond of sensuality
an4.10:9.2 | S: and the desire to be reborn in a future life; | T: and the bond of existence,
an4.10:9.3 | S: yoked also to views, | T: fettered by the bond of views,
an4.10:9.4 | S: and governed by ignorance, | T: preceded by ignorance,
an4.10:10.1 | S: sentient beings continue to transmigrate, | T: beings go on in saṁsāra,
an4.10:10.2 | S: with ongoing birth and death. | T: led on in birth and death.
an4.10:10.3 | S: But those who fully understand sensual pleasures, | T: But having entirely understood sense pleasures
an4.10:10.4 | S: and the yoke to all future lives; | T: and the bond of existence,
an4.10:11.1 | S: with the yoke of views eradicated, | T: having uprooted the bond of views
an4.10:11.2 | S: and ignorance dispelled, | T: and dissolved ignorance,
an4.10:11.3 | S: unyoked from all yokes, | T: the sages have severed all bonds;
an4.10:11.4 | S: truly those sages have slipped their yoke.” | T: they have gone beyond bondage.

