## an4.13
an4.13:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these four right efforts. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these four right strivings.
an4.13:1.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an4.13:1.3 | S: A mendicant generates enthusiasm, tries, makes an effort, exerts the mind, and strives so that bad, unskillful qualities don’t arise. | T: (1) Here, a bhikkhu generates desire for the non-arising of unarisen bad unwholesome states; he makes an effort, arouses energy, applies his mind, and strives.
an4.13:1.4 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen are given up. | T: (2) He generates desire for the abandoning of arisen bad unwholesome states; he makes an effort, arouses energy, applies his mind, and strives.
an4.13:1.5 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that skillful qualities arise. | T: (3) He generates desire for the arising of unarisen wholesome states; he makes an effort, arouses energy, applies his mind, and strives.
an4.13:1.6 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that skillful qualities that have arisen remain, are not lost, but increase, mature, and are fully developed. | T: (4) He generates desire for the persistence of arisen wholesome states, for their non-decline, increase, expansion, and fulfillment by development; he makes an effort, arouses energy, applies his mind, and strives.
an4.13:1.7 | S: These are the four right efforts. | T: These are the four right strivings.”
an4.13:2.1 | S: By rightly striving, <j>they’ve crushed Māra’s sovereignty; | T: Those who strive rightly overcome the realm of Māra;
an4.13:2.2 | S: unattached, they’ve gone beyond  <j>the peril of birth and death. | T: they are unattached, gone beyond fear of birth and death.
an4.13:2.3 | S: Contented and unstirred, they’ve vanquished Māra with his legions; | T: They are contented and unstirred, having conquered Māra and his mount;
an4.13:2.4 | S: now they’ve gone beyond all Namuci’s forces, <j>they’re happy.” | T: those happy ones have overcome all Namuci’s armies.
an4.13:2.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.14
an4.14:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these four efforts. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these four strivings.
an4.14:1.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an4.14:1.3 | S: The efforts to restrain, to give up, to develop, and to preserve. | T: Striving by restraint, striving by abandonment, striving by development, and striving by protection.
an4.14:1.4 | S: And what, mendicants, is the effort to restrain? | T: (1) “And what, bhikkhus, is striving by restraint?
an4.14:1.5 | S: When a mendicant sees a sight with their eyes, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: Here, having seen a form with the eye, a bhikkhu does not grasp its marks and features.
an4.14:1.6 | S: If the faculty of sight were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, they practice restraint, protecting the faculty of sight, and achieving its restraint. | T: Since, if he left the eye faculty unrestrained, bad unwholesome states of longing and dejection might invade him, he practices restraint over it, he guards the eye faculty, he undertakes the restraint of the eye faculty.
an4.14:1.7 | S: When they hear a sound with their ears … | T: Having heard a sound with the ear …
an4.14:1.8 | S: When they smell an odor with their nose … | T: Having smelled an odor with the nose …
an4.14:1.9 | S: When they taste a flavor with their tongue … | T: Having tasted a taste with the tongue …
an4.14:1.10 | S: When they feel a touch with their body … | T: Having felt a tactile object with the body …
an4.14:1.11 | S: When they know an idea with their mind, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: Having cognized a mental phenomenon with the mind, a bhikkhu does not grasp its marks and features.
an4.14:1.12 | S: If the faculty of mind were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, they practice restraint, protecting the faculty of mind, and achieving its restraint. | T: Since, if he left the mind faculty unrestrained, bad unwholesome states of longing and dejection might invade him, he practices restraint over it, he guards the mind faculty, he undertakes the restraint of the mind faculty.
an4.14:1.13 | S: This is called the effort to restrain. | T: This is called striving by restraint.
an4.14:2.1 | S: And what, mendicants, is the effort to give up? | T: (2) “And what is striving by abandonment?
an4.14:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t tolerate a sensual, | T: Here, a bhikkhu does not tolerate an arisen sensual thought; he abandons it, dispels it, terminates it, and obliterates it.
an4.14:2.3 | S: malicious, | T: He does not tolerate an arisen thought of ill will …
an4.14:2.4 | S: or cruel thought that’s arisen, but gives it up, dispels it, eliminates it, and obliterates it. | T: an arisen thought of harming …
an4.14:2.5 | S: They don’t tolerate any bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen, but give them up, dispel them, eliminate them, and obliterate them. | T: bad unwholesome states whenever they arise; he abandons them, dispels them, terminates them, and obliterates them.
an4.14:2.6 | S: This is called the effort to give up. | T: This is called striving by abandonment.
an4.14:3.1 | S: And what, mendicants, is the effort to develop? | T: (3) “And what is striving by development?
an4.14:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops the awakening factors of mindfulness, | T: Here, a bhikkhu develops the enlightenment factor of mindfulness, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
an4.14:3.3 | S: investigation of principles, | T: He develops the enlightenment factor of discrimination of phenomena …
an4.14:3.4 | S: energy, | T: the enlightenment factor of energy …
an4.14:3.5 | S: rapture, | T: the enlightenment factor of rapture …
an4.14:3.6 | S: tranquility, | T: the enlightenment factor of tranquility …
an4.14:3.7 | S: immersion, | T: the enlightenment factor of concentration …
an4.14:3.8 | S: and equanimity, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: the enlightenment factor of equanimity, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
an4.14:3.9 | S: This is called the effort to develop. | T: This is called striving by development.
an4.14:4.1 | S: And what, mendicants, is the effort to preserve? | T: (4) “And what is striving by protection?
an4.14:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant preserves a meditation subject that’s a fine basis of immersion: the perception of a skeleton, a worm-infested corpse, a livid corpse, a split open corpse, or a bloated corpse. | T: Here, a bhikkhu protects an arisen excellent object of concentration: the perception of a skeleton, the perception of a worm-infested corpse, the perception of a livid corpse, the perception of a festering corpse, the perception of a fissured corpse, the perception of a bloated corpse.
an4.14:4.3 | S: This is called the effort to preserve. | T: This is called striving by protection.
an4.14:4.4 | S: These are the four efforts. | T: “These, bhikkhus, are the four kinds of striving.”
an4.14:5.1 | S: Restraint and giving up, | T: Restraint and abandonment,
an4.14:5.2 | S: development and preservation: | T: development and protection:
an4.14:5.3 | S: these are the four efforts | T: these four strivings
an4.14:5.4 | S: taught by the Kinsman of the Sun. | T: were taught by the Kinsman of the Sun.
an4.14:5.5 | S: Any mendicant who keenly applies these | T: By these means an ardent bhikkhu here
an4.14:5.6 | S: may attain the ending of suffering.” | T: can attain the destruction of suffering.
an4.14:5.7 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)


